\begin{abstract}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) grounds large language models in external databases, acting as a cornerstone for knowledge-intensive AI. Accurate context assembly is vital in high-stakes domains such as law, where factual drift, anachronisms, and hallucinations carry severe operational consequences. However, existing systems struggle with multi-hop temporal precedent tracking and procedural reasoning, frequently relying on shallow keyword-matching evaluators that act merely as entity alignment detectors. To address these vulnerabilities, this paper introduces TERRA (Temporal-Evolution and Reasoning-Trace RAG), an integrated dual-phase GraphRAG architecture configured for dynamically evolving, knowledge-intensive environments. The framework constructs an offline dual-index corpus comprising an Event Evolution Graph (EEG) across \CorpusTotalOpinions\ authentic Supreme Court opinions connected by \CorpusPrecedentEdges\ directed citation edges alongside a ChromaDB procedural reasoning trace index. During online inference, a Traffic Cop router with coarse geometric domain pre-filtering dynamically maps user queries across a bimodal execution profile---routing single-point lookups through a high-speed Fast Path (median \TerraFastPathMedianSeconds~s) and multi-hop legal reasoning through an EEG citation traversal Hard Path (median \TerraHardPathMedianSeconds~s)---while an autonomous Natural Language Inference (NLI) grader enforces semantic entailment boundaries prior to generation. The architecture was evaluated across the canonical \numUniqueQueries-query legal temporal reasoning benchmark across \numComparativePipelines\ comparative pipelines (\numTotalEvals\ total evaluations, executed deterministically at temperature $T=0.0$ in a single-run protocol), alongside a \numPilotQueries-query component ablation study ($N=\numPilotRepeatedRuns$ repeated runs, \numPilotEvals\ evaluations). Experimental results demonstrate that while HippoRAG-Adapted achieves a marginally higher raw overall faithfulness score due to high unanswerable query refusal volume ($\HippoRAGOverallFaithfulnessMean$ vs. $\TerraOverallFaithfulnessMean$, with $165/350$ vs. $117/350$ refusals), TERRA GraphRAG achieves the highest Answer-Only Faithfulness among all evaluated RAG architectures ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$), decisively outperforming Flat Vector RAG ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsFlatWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsFlatDz$, $\TerraVsFlatRAGCatBWinRate$ win rate) and HippoRAG-Adapted ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDz$, $\TerraVsHippoRAGCatBWinRate$ win rate) on Category B multi-hop precedent reasoning. Furthermore, TERRA delivers a \TerraCatDRejectionRate\ safety refusal rate on adversarial deception queries (Category D), providing $97.3\%$ total safe handling ($73/75$ queries: $42/75$ refusals, $31/75$ verified refutations), with fatal errors reduced by 80\% to only $2/75$ ($2.7\%$) procedural misattributions (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}), $100.0\%$ refusal on out-of-context queries, zero false refusals on single-point factual lookups (Category A), and superior lexical precedent recall (\TerraCatBRougeRecall\ vs. \FlatRAGCatBRougeRecall). These findings demonstrate that aligning chronological citation graphs with procedural reasoning trajectories substantially improves grounded temporal multi-hop reasoning and provides an explicit epistemic safety mechanism, establishing an explainable architecture for complex legal knowledge retrieval with identified failure modes.
\end{abstract}

