\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has rapidly evolved into a foundational architecture for knowledge-intensive artificial intelligence. By explicitly decoupling the parametric memory of Large Language Models (LLMs) from dynamic external knowledge bases, RAG enables real-time factual grounding and transparent source attribution without the prohibitive computational costs of continuous model retraining. In high-stakes, knowledge-intensive environments---where factual errors and hallucinations carry severe real-world consequences---the fidelity of the retrieved context is not merely an optimization preference, but a strict operational requirement. As enterprise applications scale, the early paradigm of naive ``retrieve-then-generate'' pipelines characterized by static vector databases and fixed-length document chunking has proven structurally inadequate.

The fundamental bottleneck in modern RAG systems arises when navigating complex data landscapes characterized by continuous knowledge drift and multi-step reasoning demands. Real-world data evolves dynamically; consequently, static vector indices inevitably accumulate conflicting, time-stamped versions of the same fact. When queried, standard retrievers surface all of these conflicting versions simultaneously based on semantic similarity, leaving the frozen generative model unable to resolve the chronological authority, which induces severe temporal hallucinations. Furthermore, when processing complex logical derivations, standard pipelines retrieve fragmented textual snippets that sever causal dependencies, proving entirely insufficient for tasks that require deep, multi-hop reasoning.

Recent advancements have attempted to address these bottlenecks in isolated silos, exposing critical failure modes. While structural graph-based retrievers organize facts chronologically, they act merely as static data foundations lacking the procedural logic to guide multi-hop generative reasoning. Conversely, paradigms retrieving cognitive reasoning trajectories significantly enhance logical deduction but remain temporally blind and have been primarily restricted to quantitative domains like mathematics or coding. More critically, current Agentic RAG frameworks rely heavily on corrective evaluators that fail fundamentally on conceptual tasks. Mathematical explainability analyses, such as SHAP, have proven that standard evaluators act merely as superficial named entity alignment detectors, failing to comprehend genuine semantic relevance. Compounding this, existing query routers are largely restricted to rigid, binary-channel systems (e.g., pure dense versus pure sparse search), resulting in severe evidence-intent mismatches when unrestricted search floods the context window with noisy, irrelevant data.

To bridge these isolated gaps, this paper introduces an integrated dual-phase GraphRAG architecture purpose-built for dynamically evolving, knowledge-intensive environments. The proposed system confines all computationally heavy operations to an offline preprocessing phase, constructing a dual-index corpus that cross-links an Event Evolution Graph (EEG)---which encodes chronological causality across \CorpusTotalOpinions\ authentic judicial decisions and \CorpusPrecedentEdges\ directed citation edges---with a node-level procedural reasoning trace database that captures structured legal analysis trajectories (constitutional issue, procedural history, legal test, holding). During online inference, a small language model acts as a Traffic Cop router, dynamically allocating retrieval priority across text chunks, graph subgraphs, and procedural reasoning traces, enabling a bimodal execution profile (median \TerraFastPathMedianSeconds~s on single-point factual lookups vs. \TerraHardPathMedianSeconds~s on multi-hop precedent chains). Before the final response generation, an autonomous semantic entailment grader replaces flawed keyword-matching heuristics, verifying the true conceptual and structural relevance of the retrieved evidence.

The proposed framework was rigorously evaluated against comparative baselines, including Direct LLM generation and Flat Vector RAG, alongside component-level ablation studies utilizing comprehensive temporal and multi-perspective reasoning benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that the model significantly mitigates the attention dilution and ``context cliff'' limitations inherent in processing overly broad retrieved contexts. By seamlessly aligning chronological facts with procedural reasoning, the model substantially reduces temporal hallucinations in grounded retrieval, establishing a meaningful standard for context fidelity in high-stakes legal AI.

\subsection{Summary of Contributions}
The primary contributions of this work are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{An Authentic Dual-Index Architecture:} We design and implement an authentic dual-index judicial GraphRAG corpus that cross-links a structural Event Evolution Graph (EEG) of \CorpusTotalOpinions\ authentic SCOTUS decisions (\CorpusPrecedentEdges\ citation edges) with a node-level procedural reasoning trace database, ensuring joint chronological and logical evidence alignment with zero synthetic cases.
    \item \textbf{Traffic Cop Routing with Coarse Domain Pre-filtering:} We introduce an SLM-driven complexity router paired with a two-stage coarse proximity pre-filter ($d_{L2} < 1.3$ + Title Match) that provides scale-dependent domain boundary filtering without maintaining static allowlists (empirical coverage and calibration detailed in Section~\ref{subsec:computational-budget} and Section~\ref{subsec:firewall-analysis}).
    \item \textbf{NLI-Based Entailment Evaluation and XAI Attribution:} We replace superficial keyword-matching heuristics with a specialized Natural Language Inference (NLI) grader that enforces strict semantic entailment boundaries, providing transparent qualitative reasoning traces and dynamic hyperlink citation provenance.
    \item \textbf{Data Leakage Mitigation Framework:} We formalize explicit safeguards against temporal precedent inversion, benchmark retrieval contamination, and cross-domain feature bleeding.
    \item \textbf{Empirical Rigor and Statistical Validation:} Through extensive benchmarking (\numTotalEvals\ evaluations across \numUniqueQueries\ unique queries $\times$ \numComparativePipelines\ comparative pipelines, executed deterministically at $T=0.0$) with paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, Cohen's $d_z$ effect sizes, and 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals, we demonstrate that TERRA achieves the highest Answer-Only Faithfulness among all evaluated RAG architectures ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$ vs. HippoRAG's $\HippoRAGAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$), decisively surpasses Flat Vector RAG ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsFlatWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsFlatDz$) and HippoRAG-Adapted ($\Delta = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGWilcoxonP$, $d_z = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDz$) on Category B multi-hop reasoning, delivers a $\TerraCatDRejectionRate$ safety refusal rate on adversarial queries ($97.3\%$ total safe handling, $73/75$ queries: $42/75$ refusals, $31/75$ verified refutations), with fatal errors reduced by 80\% to $2/75$ ($2.7\%$) procedural misattributions (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}), and achieves zero false refusals on single-point factual lookups.
\end{itemize}


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}
The foundational paradigm of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) \cite{lewis2020retrieval, guu2020realm} decoupled parametric LLM memory from external knowledge bases. However, standard RAG suffers from severe vulnerabilities when managing continuous knowledge drift and multi-step reasoning. To structure retrieved evidence, recent work has pivoted toward graph-based architectures. Microsoft Research introduced GraphRAG \cite{edge2024graphrag}, which constructs hierarchical entity-relation communities for global query summarization. Similarly, Sun et al. \cite{sun2024thinkongraph} developed Think-on-Graph (ToG) to execute iterative LLM-guided reasoning over knowledge graphs. In temporal domains, Liu and Cao \cite{liu2026raglearningunderstandinglimits} introduced Chronos to track dynamic events in an Event Evolution Graph (EEG), Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2025respectingtemporalcausalconsistencyentityevent} proposed E2RAG to preserve temporal-causal consistency across entity-event subgraphs, and Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2025rightanswerrighttime} developed STAR-RAG for time-sensitive rule graph summarization. Within the legal domain, recent architectures have explored structured representations: Chen et al.~\cite{chen2026legalgraphrag} proposed LegalGraphRAG, deploying multi-agent reasoning over a tripartite legal knowledge graph (ontology, fact, and rule layers) for statutory civil and criminal law, while de Martim~\cite{demartim2025satgraphrag} introduced SAT-Graph RAG to trace diachronic statutory norm versions across formal constitutional amendments. However, these systems model codified statutory rules or formal legislative amendment events; in contrast, common-law judicial systems require tracking the longitudinal evolution of precedent across citation networks, where earlier decisions remain presumptive authority unless explicitly distinguished, limited, or overruled. Furthermore, static graph models retrieve structural summaries but lack the procedural logic required to execute multi-hop reasoning over evolving precedents.

To bridge this gap in procedural logic, recent research has explored augmenting retrieval with intermediate reasoning trajectories rather than static factual snippets alone. Li et al. \cite{li2025cotragintegratingchainthought} demonstrated this through the CoT-RAG framework, establishing that retrieving structured reasoning sub-cases and decision trees significantly mitigates logical biases compared to unstructured text retrieval. The broader transition toward System 2 agentic reasoning has been mapped by Lin et al. \cite{lin2025comprehensivesurveyreinforcementlearningbased} and Liang et al. \cite{liang2025reasoningrag12}, who detail multi-stage pipelines where autonomous agents dynamically identify knowledge gaps and generate intermediate reasoning traces to inform subsequent search actions. In parallel, the legal AI community has established rigorous evaluation benchmarks to measure procedural reasoning capabilities: CaseHOLD \cite{zheng2021casehold} evaluates judicial holding identification, while the Common Law Case Law Retrieval Benchmark (CLERC) \cite{hou2024clerc} measures multi-step precedent retrieval. These benchmarks confirm that standard lexical or dense retrievers frequently retrieve irrelevant or superseded authority unless procedural reasoning context is explicitly preserved. However, existing trace-retrieval frameworks have been predominantly evaluated on quantitative domains or static statutory collections, leaving them largely untested in longitudinal common-law environments where precedent evolution alters substantive legal authority over time.

Beyond the challenge of retrieving complex reasoning traces and temporal data, the successful integration of this multi-faceted information relies entirely on the ``connective tissue'' of the RAG pipeline: query routers and autonomous evaluators. Hashmi \cite{hashmi2026adaptivequeryroutingtierbased} highlighted the rigidity of standard single-channel retrieval, introducing an Adaptive Hybrid Retrieval framework to route queries dynamically, noting that pure vector similarity frequently fails to capture structural navigation. More critically, the evaluation mechanisms governing these routers are fundamentally flawed. Yalavarthi \cite{yalavarthi2026opensourcereproductionexplainabilityanalysis} conducted a SHAP explainability analysis on the widely adopted Corrective RAG (CRAG) architecture, mathematically proving that its standard T5-based evaluator functions purely as a named entity alignment detector. Consequently, the CRAG evaluator misclassified $88.3\%$ of conceptual science tasks as ambiguous and achieved only $5.0\%$ accuracy on non-entity queries. The real-world consequences of these shallow heuristics were exposed by Putta et al. \cite{putta2026casefactsbenchmarklegalfactchecking} via the CaseFacts benchmark, which demonstrated that augmenting models with unrestricted web search actively degrades performance in legal fact-checking compared to closed-book baselines. Because standard retrievers rely on lexical matching, they flood the context window with noisy, overruled precedents, failing to recognize when a fact has been logically overturned. These findings highlight a critical architectural gap: current systems lack the genuine Natural Language Inference (NLI) capabilities required for true semantic entailment.

Ultimately, a comprehensive review of this literature reveals that addressing these vulnerabilities in isolation is insufficient; no prior architecture unifies temporal precedent management, node-level procedural reasoning trace retrieval, and semantic-entailment evaluation into a single, cohesive pipeline. Graph models track chronological topology but lack procedural reasoning logic; trace models enhance reasoning but lack temporal awareness; and current routers rely on superficial keyword-matching that triggers catastrophic failure in high-stakes domains. The proposed architecture directly addresses this convergence gap, as summarized in the comprehensive architectural and doctrinal feature comparison of Table~\ref{tab:system_comparison}. By cross-linking a temporal Event Evolution Graph with a node-level procedural reasoning trace database, it ensures that structural precedent topology (what happened and when) and operative judicial analysis (issue, test, and holding) are retrieved as a semantically aligned pair. Furthermore, it replaces flawed entity-alignment evaluators with a SHAP-verified NLI entailment grader and utilizes a continuous three-way Traffic Cop router to dynamically balance textual, graphical, and procedural data. This design establishes a dual-phase framework capable of addressing continuous knowledge drift and multi-hop reasoning without requiring continuous foundational model retraining.

