\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental-setup}

To rigorously assess TERRA's performance in legal temporal reasoning and safety firewalling, we establish a comprehensive comparative benchmarking suite. This section outlines the benchmark query suite design, baseline architectures, and evaluation metrics.

\subsection{Benchmark Suite Design}
\label{subsec:benchmark-design}
The evaluation suite comprises an expanded benchmark of \numUniqueQueries\ unique queries evaluated across \numComparativePipelines\ comparative pipelines (\numTotalEvals\ total evaluations, executed deterministically at temperature $T=0.0$ in a single-run protocol), alongside a foundational \numPilotQueries-query pilot ablation set ($N=\numPilotRepeatedRuns$ repeated runs, \numPilotEvals\ evaluations) structured across four distinct categories designed to isolate factual accuracy, multi-hop reasoning, domain safety, and adversarial robustness. Quantitative paired text generation metrics (Faithfulness and ROUGE-L) are evaluated across in-domain queries (Categories A and B) that produce generative text outputs, while safety firewall performance is evaluated across out-of-domain and deceptive queries (Categories C and D):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Category A --- Single-Point Factual:} Single-fact retrieval queries (e.g., decision years, specific party names) evaluated against researcher-constructed ground-truth reference answers using ROUGE-L.
    \item \textbf{Category B --- Multi-Hop Evolutionary:} Complex legal queries requiring multi-decade precedent tracking across citation chains, evaluated against researcher-constructed reference answers using ROUGE-L. To test non-trivial multi-hop graph traversal while reflecting the small-world hub topology of judicial citation networks, all Category B chains are constrained to 3--5 nodes (2--4 intermediate hops), comprising 63 three-node, 32 four-node, and 5 five-node evolutionary arcs with zero 2-node direct citation pairs.
    \item \textbf{Category C --- Out-of-Context Safety:} Queries covering topics demonstrably absent from indexed U.S. Supreme Court case law (\CorpusTemporalSpan), spanning five distinct non-indexed domains ($N=15$ each, total 75): (1) Natural and Physical Sciences, (2) Foreign Constitutional and International Treaty Law (e.g., European Court of Justice, 1951 Geneva Convention, German Federal Constitutional Court), (3) Modern State and Municipal Administrative Ordinances (e.g., municipal zoning codes, city curb-cut regulations), (4) Contemporary Software Systems and Post-2020 Culture, and (5) Corporate Financial Accounting (GAAP/IFRS arithmetic). Because no controlling SCOTUS precedent exists for these inquiries, the ground-truth system requirement is strict safety refusal.
    \item \textbf{Category D --- Adversarial Deception:} Queries that embed real in-domain case titles alongside fabricated legal premises or temporal falsehoods (e.g., asserting that the 1905 \textit{Lochner} ruling was governed by the 1938 Fair Labor Standards Act, or asking for Section 5 preclearance provisions under the 1883 \textit{Civil Rights Cases}), designed to trick entity-matching routers. The acceptable safe response is either explicit safety refusal or verified substantive legal refutation that actively exposes and refutes the false premise citing authentic authority without endorsing invalidated doctrine.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Category~C Construction Protocol.} The 75 out-of-context queries were pre-registered across five distinct non-SCOTUS domains ($N=15$ each, total 75) prior to model evaluation: Subtype C1 ($N=15$): Natural and Physical Sciences (stellar nucleosynthesis, CRISPR-Cas9, Bell inequality, Carnot efficiency); Subtype C2 ($N=15$): Foreign Constitutional and International Treaty Jurisprudence (e.g., European Court of Justice, 1951 Geneva Refugee Convention, German Federal Constitutional Court); Subtype C3 ($N=15$): Modern State and Municipal Administrative Ordinances (hyper-local zoning codes, municipal curb-cut rules, and local property procedures lacking federal questions); Subtype C4 ($N=15$): Contemporary Software Systems and Post-2020 Culture (Python asyncio multiplexing, React 19 Fiber reconciler, Kubernetes scaling, modern media); and Subtype C5 ($N=15$): Corporate Financial Accounting Arithmetic (GAAP ASC 606 revenue recognition, WACC, IFRS 16 lease liability). An automated corpus-isolation audit against the 20,000-opinion indexed repository verified 0/75 case title, reporter citation, or substantive holding overlaps. All 75 queries and metadata were locked into \texttt{terra\_350\_benchmark.json} (SHA256: \texttt{8144fa81...}), and the formal domain-labeling protocol and 75-query catalog are published in supplementary documentation.

\noindent\textbf{Category~D Construction Protocol.} The 75 adversarial queries were constructed by the research team using a pre-registered four-subtype taxonomy prior to any model evaluation: Subtype D1 ($N=19$): explicit overruled precedent traps (e.g., asking to apply \textit{Plessy v. Ferguson}'s separate-but-equal doctrine as binding law); Subtype D2 ($N=19$): title-substitution and doctrinal confusion traps (swapping landmark holdings across companion cases); Subtype D3 ($N=19$): temporal inversion traps asserting chronologically impossible legal relationships, each enforced via a hard programmatic gate requiring a $\ge 5$-year historical gap verified against corpus decision dates; and Subtype D4 ($N=18$): procedural posture deceptions treating non-merits procedural orders as substantive precedent. Query sampling used \texttt{RANDOM\_SEED = 42} from the 19 verified doctrine-invalidation pairs. All queries were locked into \texttt{terra\_350\_benchmark.json} (SHA256: \texttt{8144fa81...}) before any pipeline was evaluated, with zero queries or reference answers indexed into ChromaDB, satisfying the contamination-isolation requirement of Section~\ref{subsubsec:data-leakage}.

In addition to the comparative benchmark, a standalone stress test of 15 queries (10 Out-of-Context and 5 Adversarial) is executed via \texttt{stress\_test.py} to verify firewall boundary consistency.

\subsection{Comparative Baselines}
\label{subsec:baselines}
TERRA is benchmarked against four representative baseline architectures spanning parametric and retrieval-augmented paradigms:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Direct LLM (No RAG):} The query $q$ is passed directly to the generator without retrieval. This baseline evaluates raw parametric knowledge and highlights un-gated hallucination risks.
    \item \textbf{Flat Vector RAG:} ChromaDB retrieves top-$k$ reasoning traces by semantic embedding similarity ($n_{\text{results}}=2$) and passes them directly to the generator without citation graph traversal or NLI entailment grading. This baseline isolates the specific contributions of the EEG graph and Smart Grader.
    \item \textbf{GraphRAG-Adapted:} Adapting the hierarchical entity-relation summarization framework of Microsoft GraphRAG \cite{edge2024graphrag}, this baseline partitions opinion entities into Leiden graph communities and generates responses from global community summaries, omitting directed chronological precedent edges.
    \item \textbf{HippoRAG-Adapted:} Adapting the hippocampal memory indexing architecture \cite{bernal2024hipporag}, this baseline constructs an OpenIE entity-passage knowledge graph and executes Personalized PageRank (PPR) associative diffusion seeded from query entity mentions, without typed directional citation constraints or downstream NLI self-correction gates.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Baseline Implementation Details.} All five pipelines share identical infrastructure: generator \texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct} (temperature $T=0.0$, max tokens~4,096), corpus anchor \texttt{terra\_eeg\_index\_20k.json} (20,000 opinions, SHA256: \texttt{012c999b...}), and benchmark \texttt{terra\_350\_benchmark.json} (350 queries, SHA256: \texttt{8144fa81...}). All baselines ingest from the same 145,363-edge citation network, giving each the fairest possible advantage via the verified ground-truth graph topology. GraphRAG-Adapted applies hierarchical Leiden community detection (modularity threshold $Q \ge 0.40$, domain purity $\ge 30\%$) and generates responses from local subgraph plus community summary context ($\approx$4,000--6,000 tokens). HippoRAG-Adapted executes Personalized PageRank (PPR, damping $\alpha=0.85$, convergence tolerance $10^{-6}$, max 50~iterations) over a bipartite phrase--opinion graph, returning top-$k=5$ PPR-ranked opinions. TERRA uses top-$k=2$ seed retrieval expanding via BFS to depth $d=2$ with hub degree cap~50. Payload-level parity across all pipelines was enforced programmatically, asserting identical model name, temperature, and token limit on every outgoing API call.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{subsec:eval-metrics}
System performance is evaluated across five quantitative dimensions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Faithfulness ($0.0$--$1.0$):} Evaluated via an LLM-as-a-judge framework \cite{roig2026scalablereliableevaluationai} using \texttt{databricks-meta-llama-3-3-70b-instruct} operating with an adversarial ``skeptical critic'' persona. The judge model is explicitly decoupled from the inference models to eliminate circular self-evaluation bias.
    \item \textbf{Relevance ($0.0$--$1.0$):} Scored by the LLM judge to measure query-answer directness and structural completeness.
    \item \textbf{ROUGE-L F1 and Recall (Supplementary):} Computed for Categories A and B against researcher-constructed ground-truth reference answers using the \texttt{rouge-score} library, providing an LLM-independent supplementary lexical alignment metric. Ground-truth doctrinal claims and reference holdings were cross-referenced against standard public legal reference sources (Cornell Legal Information Institute, Oyez Project, and the Congressional Research Service \textit{Constitution Annotated}) by the research team, without independent professional legal certification. ROUGE-L is reported as a supplementary metric rather than the primary measure of legal quality; the moderate judge--ROUGE correlation ($r = \JudgePearsonR$; Section~\ref{subsec:validation-methodology}) confirms that exact n-gram matching captures only a fraction of legal semantic correctness. Primary conclusions about legal quality are drawn from LLM Faithfulness scores, pairwise win rates, and safety rejection rates.
    \item \textbf{Safety Rejection and Multi-Class Robustness Rate (\%):} Percentage of out-of-domain (Category C) and adversarial deception (Category D) queries correctly neutralized. For Category D, metrics are formally decoupled into: Explicit Safety Refusal Rate ($R_{\text{ref}}$, declining to affirm the invalid prompt), Verified Substantive Refutation Rate ($R_{\text{refut}}$, actively refuting the false premise without fatal error), Total Safe Handling Rate ($R_{\text{safe}} = R_{\text{ref}} \cup R_{\text{refut}}$), Fatal Error Rate ($R_{\text{fatal}}$, credulously validating false or overruled doctrine), and Uncommitted Hedge Rate ($R_{\text{hedge}}$, equivocal non-refusal non-answers).
    \item \textbf{Stage Latency (ms):} Mean $\pm$ standard deviation execution time for routing, retrieval, NLI grading, and generation stages, recorded across all benchmark runs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Validation Methodology and Component Ablation Design}
\label{subsec:validation-methodology}
To satisfy the rigorous methodological standards required for high-stakes judicial AI, the evaluation protocol incorporates three validation controls:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Judge Calibration and Dual-Metric Alignment:} To verify that the LLM judge does not produce uncalibrated scores, we measure the correlation between the judge's Faithfulness ratings and the independent, researcher-constructed reference ROUGE-L scores across in-domain generations. Across the preliminary 35-query pilot subset ($N=\numPilotBaselineEvals$ evaluations), judge-reference correlation was $r = \PilotJudgePearsonR$ ($p = \PilotJudgePearsonP$); on the canonical \numUniqueQueries-query benchmark ($N=\numTotalEvals$ evaluations), correlation increases to Pearson $r = \JudgePearsonR$ ($p = \JudgePearsonP$; Spearman $\rho = \JudgeSpearmanRho, p = \JudgeSpearmanP$; Kendall $\tau = \JudgeKendallTau, p = \JudgeKendallP$), indicating a modest but statistically significant positive relationship between automated critic scoring and lexical grounding, though not strong enough to treat the LLM judge as a full substitute for independent human legal verification while reflecting the known divergence between exact n-gram matching and conceptual entailment.
    \item \textbf{Deterministic Single-Run Execution and Variance Control:} The canonical \numUniqueQueries-query benchmark is executed as a single deterministic run across all \numComparativePipelines\ comparative pipelines (\numTotalEvals\ total evaluations). All inference calls are pinned at temperature $T=0.0$ throughout, enforcing strict algorithmic determinism: for a fixed query and retrieved context, the generator produces identical output on every execution. Stochastic variance is therefore structurally eliminated by design rather than averaged across repeated trials. The ablation study (Section~\ref{subsec:ablation-study}) independently verified this determinism across its $N=\numPilotRepeatedRuns$ repeated runs over the 35-query pilot subset, confirming $\sigma = 0.000$ variance across all configurations.
    \item \textbf{Component-Level Ablation Matrix:} To isolate the specific performance drivers of the architecture, we formulate three same-architecture ablations alongside the full pipeline:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Full TERRA:} Traffic Cop Router + Multi-Hop EEG Traversal + NLI Smart Grader.
        \item \textbf{Ablation 1 (w/o EEG Traversal):} Replaces graph traversal with standard Flat Vector RAG while retaining NLI grading, isolating the topological contribution.
        \item \textbf{Ablation 2 (w/o NLI Smart Grader):} Disables the entailment verification and self-correction loop, generating directly from raw parametric and retrieved text to isolate firewall efficacy.
        \item \textbf{Ablation 3 (w/o Traffic Cop Router):} Forces all queries (including simple single-point factual lookups) through the full GraphRAG traversal path to quantify routing efficiency gains.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

