\subsubsection{Overall Precedent-Chain Recovery Performance}
As reported in Table~\ref{tab:retrieval_eval} (Panel A), comparative retrievers exhibit fundamental divergences in their ability to capture multi-hop common-law trajectories:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dense Semantic Search Horizon Truncation:} Flat Vector RAG achieves only $\CatBFlatMacroNodeRecall$ macro node recall ($\CatBFlatMicroNodeRecall$ micro) and captures the complete precedent trajectory on only $\CatBFlatFullPathRecall$ of queries ($\CatBFlatFullPathCount$). While dense embeddings successfully identify one or two lexically adjacent landmark cases, they almost completely fail to recover intermediate stepping-stone authorities across multi-decade evolutionary spans.
    \item \textbf{Static Community and Undirected Diffusion Limitations:} GraphRAG-Adapted achieves $\CatBGraphRAGMacroNodeRecall$ node recall and $\CatBGraphRAGFullPathRecall$ full-path recovery ($\CatBGraphRAGFullPathCount$), bounded by the fact that static Leiden modularity communities group cases topically without preserving chronological precedence. HippoRAG-Adapted achieves substantially higher node recall ($\CatBHippoRAGMacroNodeRecall$), but attains only $\CatBHippoRAGFullPathRecall$ full-chain path recall ($\CatBHippoRAGFullPathCount$). Because Personalized PageRank diffuses undirected probability mass across dense citation clusters, it frequently activates high-degree peripheral precedents rather than following the strict temporal citation spine.
    \item \textbf{Directional Precedent Spine Traversal:} In contrast, Full TERRA achieves $\CatBTerraMacroNodeRecall$ macro node recall ($\CatBTerraMicroNodeRecall$ micro) and captures the full evolutionary chain on $\CatBTerraFullPathRecall$ of queries ($\CatBTerraFullPathCount$). This represents a $\CatBTerraVsFlatNodeRecallDelta$ absolute node-recall lift and a $\CatBTerraVsFlatPathRecallFactor$ increase in complete path recovery over Flat Vector RAG, alongside a $\CatBTerraVsHippoPathRecallFactor$ complete path-recovery advantage over HippoRAG. By traversing chronologically directed citation edges and re-ranking via substantive holding headnotes, TERRA preserves the continuous chain of legal authority necessary for sound doctrinal synthesis. This empirical spectrum resolves the relative contributions of raw text, isolated traces, and graph structure: while dense retrieval over raw text chunks achieves only $\CatBFlatMacroNodeRecall$ node recall and $\CatBFlatFullPathRecall$ path recovery, and semantic vector retrieval over isolated reasoning traces drops multi-hop faithfulness to $\AblationOneCatBFaithfulness$ (Ablation 1, Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), unifying procedural traces with directed citation graphs lifts precedent node recall to $\CatBTerraMacroNodeRecall$ (\TraceTopologicalAbsLift, \TraceTopologicalRelMultiplier\ multiplier), captures $\CatBTerraFullPathRecall$ of full precedent trajectories (\TracePathAbsLift, \TracePathRelMultiplier\ multiplier), and sustains $\TerraCatBFaithfulness$ faithfulness.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Chain-Length Disaggregation and Hop Analysis}
Table~\ref{tab:retrieval_eval} (Panel B) disaggregates precedent discovery across evolutionary chain lengths, addressing Second Review Issue~\#11:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{3-Node Chains ($N=\CatBQueriesLenThree$, 2 Citation Hops, 1 Intermediate Node):} On 2-hop chains (e.g., \textit{Plessy} $\to$ \textit{Gaines} $\to$ \textit{Brown}), Flat Vector RAG captures the full chain in only $\CatBFlatPathRecallLenThree$ of queries ($\CatBFlatPathCountLenThree$), GraphRAG achieves $\CatBGraphRAGPathRecallLenThree$ ($\CatBGraphRAGPathCountLenThree$), and HippoRAG achieves $\CatBHippoRAGPathRecallLenThree$ ($\CatBHippoRAGPathCountLenThree$). Full TERRA achieves $\CatBTerraNodeRecallLenThree$ node recall and $\CatBTerraPathRecallLenThree$ full path recovery ($\CatBTerraPathCountLenThree$), confirming that $d=2$ graph expansion reliably spans two-hop doctrinal progressions.
    \item \textbf{4-Node Chains ($N=\CatBQueriesLenFour$, 3 Citation Hops, 2 Intermediate Nodes):} When doctrinal arcs span 3 citation hops (e.g., \textit{Strauder} $\to$ \textit{Neal} $\to$ \textit{Norris} $\to$ \textit{Batson}), baseline path recovery drops to zero for both Flat Vector RAG ($\CatBFlatPathRecallLenFour$, $\CatBFlatPathCountLenFour$) and GraphRAG ($\CatBGraphRAGPathRecallLenFour$, $\CatBGraphRAGPathCountLenFour$), and collapses to $\CatBHippoRAGPathRecallLenFour$ ($\CatBHippoRAGPathCountLenFour$) for HippoRAG. In contrast, Full TERRA sustains $\CatBTerraNodeRecallLenFour$ node recall and $\CatBTerraPathRecallLenFour$ full path recovery ($\CatBTerraPathCountLenFour$).
    \item \textbf{5-Node Chains ($N=\CatBQueriesLenFive$, 4 Citation Hops, 3 Intermediate Nodes):} On long 4-hop lineages, all baseline retrievers fail completely to recover the full precedent path ($\CatBFlatPathRecallLenFive$, $\CatBGraphRAGPathRecallLenFive$, and $\CatBHippoRAGPathRecallLenFive$; $0/5$ each). Full TERRA achieves $\CatBTerraNodeRecallLenFive$ node recall and $\CatBTerraPathRecallLenFive$ full path recovery ($\CatBTerraPathCountLenFive$). Due to the limited sample size ($N=\CatBQueriesLenFive$, exact Clopper-Pearson 95\% CI: \CatBFiveNodeClopperPearsonCI), this 5-node stratum serves as a qualitative descriptive diagnostic demonstrating topological feasibility rather than a statistically generalized population estimate.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Formal Definition and Seed Positioning of Multi-Seed Anchoring}
The ability of TERRA's nominal $d=2$ graph traversal to achieve complete path recovery on 3-hop and 4-hop lineages is governed by \textit{multi-seed anchoring}. For an evolutionary query $q$, entity and legal concept extraction identifies a set of seed nodes $S_q \subset V$ with $|S_q| \ge 2$ (empirical mean of $\CatBMeanSeedsPerQuery$ seeds per query across Category~B; $\CatBTwoSeedsPct$ with $|S_q|=2$, $\CatBThreeSeedsPct$ with $|S_q|=3$, and $\CatBFourPlusSeedsPct$ with $|S_q| \ge 4$). From each seed $s \in S_q$, directed EEG traversal expands a $d=2$ graph ball $B(s, 2)$ along forward and backward citation edges with breadth $k=2$.

Crucially, empirical auditing of seed placements confirms that multi-seed extraction anchors the extremities of the precedent trajectory: in $\CatBEndpointsAnchoredPct$ of 5-node chains (5/5) and $\CatBFourNodeEndpointsAnchoredPct$ of recovered 4-node chains (20/20), seeds anchor both the historical origin (index 0) and the culminating modern authority (index $m-1$). For an evolutionary chain $v_0 \to \dots \to v_{m-1}$, the forward 2-hop ball $B(v_0, 2)$ covers $\{v_0, v_1, v_2\}$ while the reverse 2-hop ball $B(v_{m-1}, 2)$ covers $\{v_{m-1}, v_{m-2}, v_{m-3}\}$. When intermediate branching along the precedent spine remains within the $k=2$ beam, the forward and backward neighborhoods intersect at intermediate stepping-stone authorities ($v_2$ in 5-node chains), seamlessly covering 3-hop and 4-hop doctrinal paths without requiring explosive unconstrained search depths ($d \ge 4$). Conversely, in the 12 non-recovered 4-node cases, high citation fan-out at intermediate nodes exceeded the $k=2$ beam width, causing the traversal to prune intermediate nodes despite correct endpoint seeding.

These empirical findings confirm that TERRA's high generative win rates (Table~\ref{tab:overall_summary}) and low fabrication rates (Table~\ref{tab:clerc_eval}) are fundamentally anchored in direct evidence retrieval quality rather than ungrounded parametric fluency. We evaluate hyperparameter robustness over depth and breadth grids ($k \in \{1,2,4\}$, $d \in \{1,2,3\}$) in Section~\ref{subsec:ablation-studies}.

