\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The empirical evaluation validates that TERRA addresses fundamental failure modes of standard RAG architectures in knowledge-intensive, dynamic environments. This section contextualizes our primary architectural contributions, candidly examines current system limitations, and outlines future research directions.

\subsection{Architectural Differentiation and System Scope}
\label{subsec:contrib-discussion}
Compared to Microsoft Research's GraphRAG framework \cite{edge2024graphrag}---which constructs static hierarchical entity-relation communities for dataset summarization---our architecture directly addresses temporal precedent evolution and knowledge drift. While Microsoft GraphRAG requires substantial offline LLM summarization costs and remains temporally blind to overruled holdings, our dual-index Event Evolution Graph (EEG) cross-links chronological citation edges (\texttt{PRECEDES}) with curated OVERRULES invalidation annotations (encoded exclusively as Category~D benchmark labels rather than graph edges; see Section~\ref{subsec:phase1-ingestion}) and node-level procedural reasoning traces. Furthermore, our NLI Smart Grader enforces strict semantic entailment, preventing hallucinations on dynamic jurisprudence where static community summaries fail \cite{asai2024selfrag, zheng2023judging}.

Three core design innovations drive TERRA's performance:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Multi-Tier Domain Boundary Safety:} Rather than relying on static allowlists, TERRA couples lexical anchoring with downstream NLI entailment gating and scale-dependent geometric filtering.
    \item \textbf{Entailment-Gated Generation and XAI Attribution:} By replacing keyword heuristics with an NLI Smart Grader, TERRA operationalizes a ``prefer silence over hallucination'' principle with transparent reasoning paths.
    \item \textbf{Citation Graphs as Temporal Reasoning Structures:} By structuring opinions as chronological citation edges (\texttt{PRECEDES}) within an Event Evolution Graph and performing OVERRULES invalidation auditing via curated benchmark annotations (Section~\ref{subsubsec:data-leakage}), TERRA explicitly models chronological precedent shifts.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Empirical Analysis of Firewall Component Contributions and Scale Dynamics}
\label{subsec:firewall-analysis}

A critical finding from our systematic post-hoc auditing concerns the empirical division of labor within TERRA's safety architecture. The original design conceptualized the Domain Boundary Firewall as an active two-stage pre-retrieval filter where vector distance ($d_{L2} < 1.3$) flags out-of-domain (OOD) queries before graph traversal. However, systematic auditing across the preliminary 50-query Category C audit set on the initial 10,009-opinion index revealed that the vector proximity gate was non-functional at this scale ($0/50$ intercepted) because unconstrained OOD distances ($0.281 \le d_{L2} \le 0.859$) fell entirely beneath $1.3$.

Consequently, safety performance in Table~\ref{tab:overall_summary} (\TerraCatCRejectionRate\ refusal on Category C, \TerraCatDRejectionRate\ refusal and $41.3\%$ verified refutation on Category D) was delivered almost entirely by the downstream NLI Smart Grader (Tier 3) via double-failure refusal. When OOD queries entered retrieval, the resulting lack of semantic entailment triggered a safe refusal. Removing NLI in ablation (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, Ablation 2) caused an immediate collapse of adversarial safety to 0.0\%, confirming NLI as the true empirical safeguard.

Crucially, empirical calibration on the expanded 20,000-opinion substantive corpus demonstrates that geometric pre-filtering exhibits scale- and density-dependent behavior. As reported in Table~\ref{tab:computational_budget} (Panel~B), evaluating the full $N=75$ Category~C benchmark suite against the 20k index yields an isolated AUROC of $\TerraGateIsolatedAUROC$. At the operational threshold $\tau = 1.30$, geometric pre-filtering achieves $\TerraGateFPRTauOneThreeZero$ in-domain false refusal ($\TerraGateFPRCountTauOneThreeZero$) while intercepting $\TerraGateTPRTauOneThreeZero$ ($\TerraGateTPRCountTauOneThreeZero$) of out-of-domain probes pre-retrieval. Comparing the 10k index (where 50/50 probes fell below $1.3$) to the 20k index (where 41/75 probes exceed $1.3$) reflects an empirical observation of corpus manifold density: expanding corpus volume across seven verified constitutional domains creates denser semantic clustering, pushing extraneous out-of-domain queries farther into the embedding margin. We do not assert any generalized theoretical scaling law from these two empirical corpus snapshots; rather, we treat geometric proximity strictly as an optional coarse pre-filter that saves retrieval and NLI compute on obvious outliers, while downstream NLI entailment verification serves as the indispensable primary safety firewall.

