\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper introduced TERRA (Temporal-Evolution and Reasoning-Trace RAG), a unified dual-phase GraphRAG architecture addressing continuous knowledge drift and multi-step reasoning demands in dynamic legal environments. By unifying an Event Evolution Graph over \CorpusTotalOpinions\ authentic SCOTUS decisions (\CorpusPrecedentEdges\ citation edges) with node-level procedural reasoning traces, TERRA enables online inference via Traffic Cop routing, BFS citation traversal, an NLI Smart Grader, and strict data leakage mitigation. Benchmark evaluation across \numUniqueQueries\ queries (\numTotalEvals\ evaluations) alongside a \numPilotQueries-query ablation study demonstrates decisive superiority on multi-hop evolutionary reasoning ($\TerraCatBFaithfulness$ vs. $\FlatRAGCatBFaithfulness$, $\Delta = \CatBTerraVsFlatDelta$, $p = \CatBTerraVsFlatWilcoxonP$; and vs. HippoRAG's $\HippoRAGCatBFaithfulness$, $\Delta = \CatBTerraVsHippoRAGDelta$, $p < 10^{-10}$), highest Answer-Only Faithfulness among all evaluated RAG systems ($\TerraAnswerOnlyFaithfulness$), an adversarial safety refusal rate of \TerraCatDRejectionRate\ ($97.3\%$ total safe handling, $73/75$ queries: $42/75$ refusals, $31/75$ refutations), with fatal errors reduced by 80\% to $2/75$ ($2.7\%$) procedural misattributions (Section~\ref{subsec:limitations}), zero false refusals on single-point factual lookups (Category A), and fully traceable XAI citation provenance. These results demonstrate that aligning chronological citation graphs with procedural reasoning trajectories substantially improves grounded temporal multi-hop reasoning and provides an explicit epistemic safety mechanism, establishing an explainable architectural foundation for complex legal knowledge retrieval with identified failure modes \cite{edge2024graphrag, sun2024thinkongraph, zheng2023judging}.
